## Adapter vs Facade

```
Facade:  simplifies — wraps complex internals with a clean API
Adapter: translates — converts an incompatible external interface
```

## Composite Pattern

## Intent

Compose objects into tree structures to represent part-whole hierarchies, so clients treat individual objects and composites uniformly.

## Classic Implementation

```typescript
interface FileSystemNode {
  name: string;
  getSize(): number;
  print(indent?: string): void;
}

// Leaf
class File implements FileSystemNode {
  constructor(public name: string, private size: number) {}
  getSize() { return this.size; }
  print(indent = '') { console.log(`${indent}📄 ${this.name} (${this.size}KB)`); }
}

// Composite — contains children that are FileSystemNodes
class Directory implements FileSystemNode {
  private children: FileSystemNode[] = [];

  constructor(public name: string) {}

  add(node: FileSystemNode) { this.children.push(node); }
  remove(node: FileSystemNode) {
    this.children = this.children.filter(c => c !== node);
  }

  getSize() { return this.children.reduce((s, c) => s + c.getSize(), 0); }

  print(indent = '') {
    console.log(`${indent}📁 ${this.name}`);
    this.children.forEach(c => c.print(indent + '  '));
  }
}

// Usage — client treats File and Directory the same
const src = new Directory('src');
const components = new Directory('components');
components.add(new File('Button.tsx', 4));
components.add(new File('Input.tsx', 6));
src.add(components);
src.add(new File('index.ts', 1));

src.print();
// 📁 src
//   📁 components
//     📄 Button.tsx (4KB)
//     📄 Input.tsx (6KB)
//   📄 index.ts (1KB)

console.log(src.getSize()); // 11 — recursive sum
```

## React Component Tree IS a Composite

```tsx
// Button (leaf) and Form (composite) both accept children, render JSX
// The React reconciler treats them uniformly

function Button({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  return <button>{children}</button>;
}

function Form({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  return <form>{children}</form>;
}

// Composite tree
<Form>
  <Input />
  <Button>Submit</Button> {/* leaf inside composite */}
</Form>
```

## Recursive Menu Example

```typescript
interface MenuItem {
  label: string;
  children?: MenuItem[];
}

function Menu({ items }: { items: MenuItem[] }) {
  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map(item => (
        <li key={item.label}>
          {item.label}
          {item.children && <Menu items={item.children} />} {/* recursive! */}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
// Leaf MenuItem and composite MenuItem treated identically
```

## When to Use

```
✅ Tree-structured data: file systems, org charts, comment threads
✅ Recursive UI: nested menus, accordion sections, folder trees
✅ Treating single items and collections uniformly
```

## Design Patterns in React

## Structural Patterns

### Decorator → Higher-Order Component

```typescript
// withAuth wraps any component without modifying it
function withAuth<P extends object>(Component: React.ComponentType<P>) {
  return function AuthGuard(props: P) {
    const { user } = useAuth();
    if (!user) return <Navigate to="/login" />;
    return <Component {...props} />;
  };
}

// withErrorBoundary, withSuspense, withAnalytics — all Decorators
const ProtectedDashboard = withAuth(Dashboard);
```

### Composite → Component Tree

```tsx
// Card (composite) and Icon (leaf) share the same JSX interface
<Card>
  <Icon name="star" />   {/* leaf */}
  <Text>Hello</Text>    {/* leaf */}
  <Card>                {/* nested composite */}
    <Button>OK</Button>
  </Card>
</Card>
```

### Proxy → React.lazy

```typescript
// Proxy intercepts the first render, loads the real module
const HeavyChart = React.lazy(() => import('./HeavyChart'));
// Before the module loads, React renders the Suspense fallback
// After — renders the real HeavyChart
```

### Facade → Custom Hooks

```typescript
// Hides TanStack Query, error normalisation, cache keys
